# CHANGELOG.md is append-mostly: nearly every feat/fix PR adds one bullet under
# `## [Unreleased]` (gate-changelog enforces it). Concurrent branches therefore
# conflict on it constantly, and a conflict here aborts cw's fix-dispatch merge
# of origin/main into a worker branch, killing the review fix loop. Git's
# built-in union driver keeps both sides' lines instead of conflicting. The
# known cost: if both sides add the same `### Section` heading, it can appear
# twice; tidy that when cutting a release.
#
# Relationship to .claude/scripts/classify_merge_conflict.py (#1850): its
# `doc_append` category also resolves CHANGELOG conflicts, but only where
# /auto-dev-finalize invokes it on a post-push rebase. This attribute is
# authoritative for CHANGELOG.md in every git merge/rebase (fix-dispatch
# included), so CHANGELOG.md conflicts no longer reach that script. It still
# owns `docs/` appends. Change one mechanism, reconsider the other.
#
# GitHub ignores merge drivers, so an open PR still shows CONFLICTING/DIRTY
# for a CHANGELOG-only conflict that a local rebase silently unions (after a
# release, wrongly). The `repair_changelog_conflict` review recipe (#2771,
# src/cw/reconcile/review_recipes/changelog_repair.py) repairs such PRs by
# rebuilding the file structurally instead of trusting this driver's output;
# changing this attribute changes what its conflict detector must derive.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
